WHAT ARE SET OPERATORS?
-----------------------
Set operators are used to combine results from two or more SELECT statements.
They work on result sets.

================================================================================
TYPES OF SET OPERATORS
================================================================================

Main set operators in SQL:
  1. UNION
  2. UNION ALL
  3. INTERSECT
  4. EXCEPT / MINUS

================================================================================
IMPORTANT RULES FOR SET OPERATORS
================================================================================

Both queries must have:
  1. Same number of columns
  2. Same datatype columns
  3. Same column order

================================================================================
EXAMPLE TABLES
================================================================================

Table A:
+----+-------+
| id | name  |
+----+-------+
| 1  | Ravi  |
| 2  | Priya |
| 3  | Arun  |
+----+-------+

Table B:
+----+-------+
| id | name  |
+----+-------+
| 2  | Priya |
| 3  | Arun  |
| 4  | Kiran |
+----+-------+

================================================================================
1. UNION
================================================================================

Definition: Combines results and removes duplicate rows.

Syntax:
  SELECT column_name FROM table1
  UNION
  SELECT column_name FROM table2;

Example:
  SELECT name FROM TableA
  UNION
  SELECT name FROM TableB;

Output:
+-------+
| name  |
+-------+
| Ravi  |
| Priya |
| Arun  |
| Kiran |
+-------+

Note: Duplicate values (Priya, Arun) are removed.

UNION Execution (Step-by-Step):
  Step 1: Execute first SELECT query -> Ravi, Priya, Arun
  Step 2: Execute second SELECT query -> Priya, Arun, Kiran
  Step 3: Combine results.
  Step 4: Remove duplicate rows.
  Step 5: Return final output.

Diagram:
  Query 1 Result
       ↓
  Query 2 Result
       ↓
  Combine Results
       ↓
  Remove Duplicates
       ↓
  Final Output

================================================================================
2. UNION ALL
================================================================================

Definition: Combines results including duplicates.

Syntax:
  SELECT column_name FROM table1
  UNION ALL
  SELECT column_name FROM table2;

Example:
  SELECT name FROM TableA
  UNION ALL
  SELECT name FROM TableB;

Output:
+-------+
| name  |
+-------+
| Ravi  |
| Priya |
| Arun  |
| Priya |
| Arun  |
| Kiran |
+-------+

Duplicates are NOT removed.

UNION vs UNION ALL:
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| UNION                    | UNION ALL                |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| Removes duplicates       | Keeps duplicates         |
| Slower                   | Faster                   |
| Uses sorting/distinct    | No duplicate checking    |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+

UNION ALL Execution:
  Step 1: Execute first query.
  Step 2: Execute second query.
  Step 3: Append results directly.
  Step 4: Return all rows.

Diagram:
  Query 1
     ↓
  Query 2
     ↓
  Append Results
     ↓
  Final Output

================================================================================
3. INTERSECT
================================================================================

Definition: Returns only common rows from both queries.

Syntax:
  SELECT column_name FROM table1
  INTERSECT
  SELECT column_name FROM table2;

Example:
  SELECT name FROM TableA
  INTERSECT
  SELECT name FROM TableB;

Output:
+-------+
| name  |
+-------+
| Priya |
| Arun  |
+-------+

Only matching rows returned.

INTERSECT Execution:
  Step 1: Execute both queries.
  Step 2: Compare rows.
  Step 3: Keep common rows only.
  Step 4: Return matching rows.

Diagram:
  Query 1 Rows
       ↓
  Compare
       ↓
  Query 2 Rows
       ↓
  Common Rows Returned

================================================================================
4. EXCEPT / MINUS
================================================================================

Definition: Returns rows from first query not present in second query.

Important:
  +----------------------+---------+
  | Database             | Keyword |
  +----------------------+---------+
  | SQL Server/PostgreSQL| EXCEPT  |
  | Oracle               | MINUS   |
  +----------------------+---------+

Syntax:
  SELECT column_name FROM table1
  EXCEPT
  SELECT column_name FROM table2;

Example:
  SELECT name FROM TableA
  EXCEPT
  SELECT name FROM TableB;

Output:
+------+
| name |
+------+
| Ravi |
+------+

Rows existing only in first query.

EXCEPT Execution:
  Step 1: Execute first query.
  Step 2: Execute second query.
  Step 3: Compare rows.
  Step 4: Remove matching rows.
  Step 5: Return remaining rows.

Diagram:
  Query 1 Rows
       ↓
  Remove Query 2 Matches
       ↓
  Remaining Rows Returned

================================================================================
REAL-TIME EXAMPLE – ONLINE STORE CUSTOMERS
================================================================================

Amazon Customers:           Flipkart Customers:
+-------+                  +-------+
| name  |                  | name  |
+-------+                  +-------+
| Ravi  |                  | Priya |
| Priya |                  | Arun  |
| Arun  |                  | Kiran |
+-------+                  +-------+

UNION:      All customers (Ravi, Priya, Arun, Kiran)
INTERSECT:  Common customers (Priya, Arun)
EXCEPT:     Amazon-only customers (Ravi)

================================================================================
ORDER BY WITH SET OPERATORS
================================================================================

ORDER BY comes at the end of the entire statement.

Example:
  SELECT name FROM TableA
  UNION
  SELECT name FROM TableB
  ORDER BY name;

================================================================================
SQL EXECUTION ORDER FOR SET OPERATORS
================================================================================

Query:
  SELECT name FROM TableA
  UNION
  SELECT name FROM TableB;

Execution Flow:
  Step 1: Execute first SELECT.
  Step 2: Execute second SELECT.
  Step 3: Apply set operator.
  Step 4: Remove duplicates if needed (UNION).
  Step 5: Return final result.

================================================================================
DIFFERENCE BETWEEN JOIN AND SET OPERATORS
================================================================================

+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| JOIN                     | Set Operators            |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| Combines columns         | Combines rows            |
| Uses related columns     | Uses query results       |
| Horizontal merge         | Vertical merge           |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+

================================================================================
PERFORMANCE
================================================================================

+------------+-------------+
| Operator   | Performance |
+------------+-------------+
| UNION      | Slower      |
| UNION ALL  | Faster      |
+------------+-------------+

Reason: UNION removes duplicates (requires sorting/hashing).

================================================================================
ADVANTAGES AND DISADVANTAGES
================================================================================

Advantages:
  1. Combines multiple query results
  2. Easy reporting
  3. Useful for comparisons
  4. Simple syntax

Disadvantages:
  1. Column count must match
  2. Datatypes must match
  3. UNION can be slower

================================================================================
QUICK REVISION
================================================================================

+--------------+-----------------------------------+
| Operator     | Purpose                           |
+--------------+-----------------------------------+
| UNION        | Combine + remove duplicates       |
| UNION ALL    | Combine + keep duplicates         |
| INTERSECT    | Common rows                       |
| EXCEPT/MINUS | Difference rows (first minus second) |
+--------------+-----------------------------------+

Memory Trick:
  UNION       → Combine
  UNION ALL   → Combine everything
  INTERSECT   → Common rows
  EXCEPT      → Remove matching rows

================================================================================
INTERVIEW POINTS
================================================================================

1. Set operators combine query results vertically (row-wise).
2. UNION removes duplicates.
3. UNION ALL keeps duplicates (faster).
4. INTERSECT returns common rows.
5. EXCEPT/MINUS returns rows from first query not in second.
6. Column count and datatype must match between queries.
7. UNION ALL performs better than UNION.
8. ORDER BY can only be used at the end of the compound query.
